{"authored" [{"question" "q-325901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Complete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola $2{x}^{2}-8{y}^{2}=16$ in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$x=2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,$\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/state.json" "file_sha256" "9498ce042edb0a5c96d12e171fe355c0be6abe32cb86f67ac5e410e6988911aa" "basis" 1793 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-325901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation of our hyperbola in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-8{y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ \\frac{2{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{8{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have $a=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}$ and $b=\\sqrt{2}.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/state.json" "file_sha256" "9498ce042edb0a5c96d12e171fe355c0be6abe32cb86f67ac5e410e6988911aa" "basis" 1793 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-325901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c35c5c96549a22aec3fa395d4dac4dd4019a49651a81abae6a45792d1ad3398a" "basis" 1793 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-325901" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/state.json" "file_sha256" "9498ce042edb0a5c96d12e171fe355c0be6abe32cb86f67ac5e410e6988911aa" "basis" 1793 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-325901" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2}\\tan(t)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/state.json" "file_sha256" "9498ce042edb0a5c96d12e171fe355c0be6abe32cb86f67ac5e410e6988911aa" "basis" 1793 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-325985" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/5b133ac885861d82704a6d6c2d99b8e4179210e4f28e94bc6d7283da23f97042.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nDetermine the domain of the parameter $t$ for this quarter, assuming that the entire parabola is defined only for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\n$t\\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/state.json" "file_sha256" "9498ce042edb0a5c96d12e171fe355c0be6abe32cb86f67ac5e410e6988911aa" "basis" 1793 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-325985" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/6ac31692404208d80637291fb30be028a7efdc3a172af0337da26039f1e87326.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get only the quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/state.json" "file_sha256" "9498ce042edb0a5c96d12e171fe355c0be6abe32cb86f67ac5e410e6988911aa" "basis" 1793 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-325985" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c35c5c96549a22aec3fa395d4dac4dd4019a49651a81abae6a45792d1ad3398a" "basis" 1793 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-325985" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/state.json" "file_sha256" "9498ce042edb0a5c96d12e171fe355c0be6abe32cb86f67ac5e410e6988911aa" "basis" 1793 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-325985" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/state.json" "file_sha256" "9498ce042edb0a5c96d12e171fe355c0be6abe32cb86f67ac5e410e6988911aa" "basis" 1793 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7883" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What are the parametric equations of the hyperbola $\\displaystyle\\frac{x^2}{49} - \\dfrac{y^2}{9} = 1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/example-7883.json" "file_sha256" "0da9bf1a4f83b743f6b86cec1cab470d48aa5c08d625720d8cd5213f8a68dc04" "basis" 1793 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7883" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{a^2} - \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\sec{t},\\quad y=b\\tan{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a = \\sqrt{49} = 7$ and $b =\\sqrt9= 3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=7\\sec{t},\\quad y=3\\tan{t}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/example-7883.json" "file_sha256" "0da9bf1a4f83b743f6b86cec1cab470d48aa5c08d625720d8cd5213f8a68dc04" "basis" 1793 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5378" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the asymptotes of the hyperbola below are $y=\\pm\\dfrac{5}{7}x,$ find the parametric equations of the hyperbola.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/fd2618e961c15a9b0665e73e18544ab08b0d611b962bc3771558c61450cf49e5.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/example-5378.json" "file_sha256" "569308a8a9290738ee5fa7004414dc72579506fb4a091c30d33819d18b7e94a3" "basis" 1793 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5378" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\sec{t},\\quad y=b\\tan{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(\\pm a, 0).$ From the graph, we have that $(7,0)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=7.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola are $y=\\pm \\dfrac{b}{a}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y=\\pm\\dfrac57x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{b}{a} & =\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ \\frac{b}{7} & =\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ b & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=7\\sec{t},\\quad y=5\\tan{t}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/example-5378.json" "file_sha256" "569308a8a9290738ee5fa7004414dc72579506fb4a091c30d33819d18b7e94a3" "basis" 1793 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5379" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola with the parametric equations $x = 6\\sec{t},\\, y = 4 \\sqrt{2}\\tan{t}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/example-5379.json" "file_sha256" "4038793e4b5799511d446578f0d430accc70bcb98c755682d84498b9e7a1298a" "basis" 1793 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5379" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\sec t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ {(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2} & ={(\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\tan{t}$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ {(\\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{2}})}^{2} & ={(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{(4\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{32} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity $\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{32}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{32} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/example-5379.json" "file_sha256" "4038793e4b5799511d446578f0d430accc70bcb98c755682d84498b9e7a1298a" "basis" 1793 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/6e12d114aedfd6c5c8ab5830e052fcc1dd3c937b825665a0490b42ca0c87972c.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx = \\sqrt2\\sec{t}, \\qquad y = \\sqrt3\\tan{t}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/example-7896.json" "file_sha256" "18e3aae9462394c6b5d87518635e0f4ec20cfadcacb052feead57bd9c892e2ff" "basis" 1793 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt2 \\sec{t}, \\quad\ny = \\sqrt3 \\tan{t}, \\quad\nt \\in [-\\pi,\\pi], \\quad\nt \\neq \\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}2.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/730325137cfca58eb17d65103e539d084f81d7ab810628261125c29dbb8a0284.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $\\left[0, \\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right)$ we get the required quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt2\\sec{t}, \\quad\ny = \\sqrt3\\tan{t}, \\quad\nt \\in \\left[0, \\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/example-7896.json" "file_sha256" "18e3aae9462394c6b5d87518635e0f4ec20cfadcacb052feead57bd9c892e2ff" "basis" 1793 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/assets/a2f0f570ecc68de1a5b34fb3bd1b341fc83a728b026b035dde848dbf986e441b.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/state.json" "file_sha256" "2372dd295512af0f6544be7c4abd582c3f1a36117b84d6c530addae38bce77e1" "basis" 1868 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/assets/e7ef7fd90c828767359daf69f68f406d61e3f82ad86b1f6b3a9480c99dedadf8.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0],$ we get the quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/state.json" "file_sha256" "2372dd295512af0f6544be7c4abd582c3f1a36117b84d6c530addae38bce77e1" "basis" 1868 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89347" "field" nil